==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|taste|-y|id2=adjectival}}. The Philippine sense is due to a {{glossary|genericized trademark}} from Taystee Bread Company, a [[defunct]] [[American]] company.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈteɪsti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tasty.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪsti|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{senseid|en|having a good flavor, appealing to the tastebuds}} Having a pleasant or satisfying [[flavor]].
#: {{syn|en|delicious|Thesaurus:delicious}}
#: {{ant|en|nontasty|untasty|Thesaurus:delicious}}
#: {{ux|en|You could make this '''tasty''' meal for breakfast.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Gregory McNamee|title=Does spinach make you strong? Ask Popeye – and science|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/07/01/health/spinach-strong-muscles-myth-facts-wellness|date=2021-07-01|passage=Now, with all these provisos in mind, what’s the best way to put spinach to work? All due respect to Popeye, a can may not be the '''tastiest''' way to introduce it to your table. {{...}} Lightly sautéing fresh spinach in a drizzle of olive or avocado oil meets this baseline, and it’s '''tasty''' and nutritious as well.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260101221623/https://www.cnn.com/2021/07/01/health/spinach-strong-muscles-myth-facts-wellness|archivedate=1 January 2026}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Rebecca Wallwork|title=‘You had to be there’: Foods that taste better on home turf|url=https://www.cnn.com/travel/foods-that-dont-travel-well|date=2023-06-15|passage=As Trevor Corson, author of “The Story of Sushi” explains, in Japan, sushi is typically eaten at a bar, where it’s customary for the customer to chat with the chef, who can recommend what’s in season. The short window during which the fish is fresh also makes it more likely to be '''tastier''' in Japan.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20231222140829/https://www.cnn.com/travel/foods-that-dont-travel-well|archivedate=22 December 2023}}
# {{senseid|en|tasteful, having or showing good taste}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} Having or showing good [[taste]]; [[tasteful]].
#: {{ant|en|untasteful|Thesaurus:gaudy}}
#: {{ux|en|These items will make an attractive and '''tasty''' display.}}
# {{senseid|en|sexy, being sexually appealing}} {{lb|en|slang}} Appealing; when applied to persons, sexually appealing.
#: {{syn|en|sexy|Thesaurus:sexy}}
# {{senseid|en|skillful}} {{lb|en|UK|informal}} [[skillful|Skillful]]; highly [[competent]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:skillful}}
# {{senseid|en|potentially violent}} {{lb|en|UK|informal}} [[potentially|Potentially]] [[violent]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2011
|author=James Robinson
|title=The Larry Diaries: Downing Street - The First 100 Days
|isbn=9780857207630
|page=
|passage=I watched it all unfold live on Sky News. Ed Miliband was there, but so were the usual bunch of troublemakers and I, for one, was expecting it to kick off.¶ Sure enough, it all got a bit '''tasty''' in the afternoon at about the time I should have been polishing off my tinned tuna. Only there was no one about to dish out my grub.¶ They were all glued to the telly watching a gang of blokes wearing balaclavas smash the windows of a Porsche showroom in Park Lane.
}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2012
|author=Craig Summers
|title=Bodyguard: My Life on the Front Line
|isbn=9781849542234
|page=
|passage=No, I wouldn't take a bullet for him, or any of them,I wasn't paid enough to go that far, but I would break up a scrap if it all got a bit '''tasty''' and, yeah, it was a great at the bar that night getting the drinks in.
}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=2014-09-05
|author=
|title=Honest scrappers
|newspaper=w:BBC News
|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/blogs-magazine-monitor-29077696
|passage=These empires of rusting metal have long been portrayed in film, fiction and TV as a haunt of the wide boy, the '''tasty''' geezer, and many other variants of ne'er-do-well|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250124104713/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/blogs-magazine-monitor-29077696|archivedate=24 January 2025}}
# {{lb|en|UK|dialect|archaic}} Having good taste; [[tasteful]].
#* {{RQ:Eliot Mill on the Floss|text=But you must wear pink, my dear; that blue thing as your aunt Glegg gave you turns you into a crowflower. Jane never ''was'' '''tasty'''.}}

